// PARAM: --analysis interval --solver warrow-per-origin
// FM 2026 Sect. 5.3, "Recovering Precision at Globals: Update Rules" --
// the paper's first improvement over widening a global's accumulated
// value: keep the contributions separate by origin (the local that
// supplied them), widen per origin, and take the join of all origins as
// the global's value. The paper states this is "sufficient to recover a
// precise result in Example 1", since there each origin contributes at
// most one value and so no widening is needed.
//
// Same program as known-imprecision/02-whole_global_widening.vimp, which
// is that same slot under plain Apinis warrowing. Each of the three call
// sites is its own origin holding one constant, so nothing widens and the
// slot reads the join of the three -- the paper's [-17,42] for g.
//
// Both bounds are pinned, not just the paper's error condition: -18 < x
// fails under warrowing for the same reason x < 43 does, so proving the
// two together is what distinguishes the recovered [-17,42] from any
// interval merely narrow enough to decide the error condition.

void f(int32 x) {
  __voblint_check(x < 43);      // PROVED
  __voblint_check(-18 < x)      // PROVED
}
void main() {
  f(1);
  f(-17);
  f(42)
}
